1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:06,592
Brush away the soil. A heavy stone appears, chipped on both faces to a point.

2
00:00:06,592 --> 00:00:14,634
Someone once held this in the hand, more than a million years ago. It comes from India, long before farms, towns or writing.

3
00:00:14,634 --> 00:00:17,432
Who made it, and what did they eat?

4
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:35,464
How do we know? There is no writing at all in this age.

5
00:00:35,464 --> 00:00:42,479
The only evidence is what people left behind: stone tools, animal bones, and the layers of soil that hold them.

6
00:00:42,479 --> 00:00:46,493
Archaeologists dug these up, and dated the layers.

7
00:00:59,017 --> 00:01:06,696
In 1863, a geologist named Robert Bruce Foote picked up a stone tool at Pallavaram, near Madras.

8
00:01:06,696 --> 00:01:13,338
It was the first Palaeolithic tool found in India. The earliest period is the Lower Palaeolithic.

9
00:01:13,338 --> 00:01:20,749
Textbooks date its start to about two million years ago, and the oldest dated tools at Attirampakkam are over a million years old.

10
00:01:20,749 --> 00:01:25,614
People made hand-axes, cleavers and choppers from quartzite, a hard stone.

11
00:01:25,614 --> 00:01:30,404
Sites include Attirampakkam in Tamil Nadu and the Soan valley in Pakistan.

12
00:01:30,404 --> 00:01:37,126
These people lived by hunting and gathering, and moved with the seasons. They had no pottery and no farms.

13
00:01:37,126 --> 00:01:42,851
They sheltered in caves and rock shelters, such as those at Bhimbetka in Madhya Pradesh.

14
00:01:42,851 --> 00:01:48,173
In the Middle Palaeolithic, from about one hundred thousand years ago, tools grew smaller.

15
00:01:48,173 --> 00:01:57,773
People made flakes, scrapers and points. The Upper Palaeolithic begins about forty thousand years ago. Tools are now thin blades.

16
00:01:57,773 --> 00:02:01,669
Modern humans, called Homo sapiens, appear.

17
00:02:01,669 --> 00:02:08,014
All of this happened in the Ice Age, called the Pleistocene, when the climate swung between cold and warm.

18
00:02:08,014 --> 00:02:12,187
The next episode follows people who learnt to grow food.

19
00:02:24,683 --> 00:02:34,777
Old name, new name. Textbooks often say Old Stone Age. The scholarly name is Palaeolithic, from Greek words meaning old stone.

20
00:02:34,777 --> 00:02:38,175
It is the same period, with two names.

21
00:02:50,683 --> 00:03:01,857
Carve this tip into memory. Lower: chopper and hand-axe. Middle: flake. Upper: blade. Three tool types, three ages.

22
00:03:01,857 --> 00:03:05,796
Remember that, and the periods are easy to place.

23
00:03:18,317 --> 00:03:35,264
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name this heavy stone tool, chipped on both faces. The answer: a hand-axe. Number two.

24
00:03:35,264 --> 00:03:48,922
This tool is smooth and ground, not chipped. Is it Palaeolithic or Neolithic? The answer: Neolithic. Number three.

25
00:03:48,922 --> 00:04:02,255
Name this kind of natural cave home, as found at Bhimbetka. The answer: a rock shelter. Now one line for Mains.

26
00:04:02,255 --> 00:04:06,673
Describe the way of life of the Palaeolithic people of India.

27
00:04:23,183 --> 00:04:31,330
The hand-axe goes into its case. It is the first exhibit in the gallery, and the oldest. Three facts to take away.

28
00:04:31,330 --> 00:04:41,856
Quartzite, shaped on both faces. First found at Pallavaram, 1863. Lower Palaeolithic: over 1 million years ago.

29
00:04:41,856 --> 00:04:45,352
Next gallery: The First Farmers.

